WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.460
(Toilet flushing).

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.440
SIERRA: Toilets.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.080
Out of sight, out of mind, until one can’t
be found.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.910
But just like healthcare and nutrition, access
to a clean toilet has enormous health consequences.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:22.000
More than a million people die every year
from diseases tied to poor sanitation, and

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.050
millions more suffer illness, stunting and
lifetime debilitations.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:33.070
All told, fifty-five percent of the global
population lacks access to safe sanitation.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.520
And for those trying to solve this problem,
the biggest barrier may not be funding, or

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.800
technology, but...culture.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.870
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:44.870
Today...toilets.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:51.670
United Nations: November the 19th is World
Toilet Day.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:56.710
TIME: The day aims to end the global sanitation
crisis.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:01.079
United Nations: This is a situation for billions
of people around the world.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:07.060
SIERRA: So, World Toilet Day is coming up
and the response to hearing that there is

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:14.530
a serious UN sponsored day for toilets might
make people laugh, or feel a little iffy on

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:15.530
discussing it.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.180
So why would we need a day like that?

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.990
YAMAKOSHI: Well, what would you do if you
didn't have a toilet?

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.320
SIERRA: Fair point.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.670
This is Brooke Yamakoshi.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:29.390
She is a water, sanitation, and hygiene specialist
at the United Nations Children's Fund, also

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.760
known as UNICEF, based in New York.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.390
She knows a lot about toilets.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.680
YAMAKOSHI: I mean, the ability to manage our
bodily functions, and these things that we

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.090
frequently don't talk about, but are so core
to us, bodily functions of urination, defecation,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.820
and for half the population, menstruation.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.409
It's really at the core of our dignity.

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:53.290
It's also a foundation for health.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:57.590
Without a toilet that contains waste, and
then separates it from people coming in contact

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.520
with that waste, we would all be exposed to
harmful pathogens that cause many different

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.810
illnesses and diseases.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.729
And it doesn't stop at the toilet either.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:10.530
So without waste being safely transported
away from our toilets and from our homes and

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.959
treated somewhere, we would all be surrounded
by wastewater in our neighborhoods and in

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:16.360
the environment.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:22.959
SIERRA: So in terms of the numbers, can you
sort of give me a wide angle view of toilet

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:25.220
access as a global issue?

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.920
SLAYMAKER: Well there are three, main things
that we measure at the global level.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.950
SIERRA: This is Tom Slaymaker.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:37.310
He also focuses on global monitoring of drinking
water, sanitation, and hygiene at UNICEF headquarters

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.379
in New York.

00:02:38.379 --> 00:02:41.819
He sees the big picture of how this plays
out around the world.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:44.200
He spoke to us on a rainy day from London.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.920
SLAYMAKER: So one is the population who practice
open defecation.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:54.510
So that means they don't have any kind of
toilet at all and they just use, you know,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.030
bushes, fields, beaches, or watercourses.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:02.459
The other thing that we measure is the population
with a basic sanitation service.

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:08.760
So that means that they have some kind of
hygienic toilet that is not being shared with

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.920
other people, that's not shared with other
households.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.730
And then the next level of service up is what
we call “safely managed sanitation.”

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:21.650
And that means that you not only have a hygienic
toilet, but you also have a mechanism in place

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:27.390
to ensure that the human waste that's produced
is then being treated and disposed of safely

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.159
before being discharged back into the environment.

00:03:30.159 --> 00:03:33.080
SIERRA: And how many people fall into each
of these categories?

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:40.460
SLAYMAKER: So the global level, we still have
673 million people practicing open defecation.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:46.319
There are about 2 billion people who still
lack even a basic level of service.

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:49.040
So that's one in four people worldwide.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:56.310
And if you look at the population without
safe sanitation, that's more like 4.2 billion

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.310
people.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.950
SIERRA: That is more than half the people
on earth.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.879
And each of them runs the risk of getting
sick, every day.

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:03.879
Access to sanitation is a problem across the
globe - even in North America - but the problem

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:04.879
is particularly dire in parts of South Asia,
Southeast Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:05.879
It tends to be worse in poor areas with rapid
population growth.

00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:09.810
SIERRA: Do we have an idea of how many people
die as a result of this problem?

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:17.310
SLAYMAKER: WHO have estimated that globally,
around 1.2 million deaths could have been

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:23.020
prevented through access to adequate drinking
water, sanitation, and hygiene.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.320
The problem is particularly acute for young
children who are very vulnerable.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:35.479
And so, I think we estimate there are around
300,000 children under five who die each year

00:04:35.479 --> 00:04:41.750
as a result of not having basic water, sanitation
and hygiene facilities.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.690
SIERRA: So when you look at a map, where do
you see this happening?

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:51.669
SLAYMAKER: Well, some regions and countries
have much further to go.

00:04:51.669 --> 00:04:56.650
If you look at our data you'll see that particularly
Sub Saharan Africa, and Southern Asia, and

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:04.070
also Oceania, the Pacific, have much lower
levels of coverage of sanitation facilities.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:07.290
And this is partly to do with different stages
of development.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.940
These are mostly poorer countries.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.210
But even if you move up the sanitation ladder,
and you start to look at issues of treatment

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:19.889
and disposal of waste, even in Europe and
North America, and in Australia and New Zealand,

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:24.199
we're still only at about three quarters of
the population that has this high level of

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:25.220
service.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:31.819
So all countries have further to go in in
order to improve sanitation, but obviously

00:05:31.819 --> 00:05:34.040
they’re all starting at different positions.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:38.490
YAMAKOSHI: Even here in the US, there are
many people that still lack safe sanitation.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:43.419
And that's not just toilets, but waste being
transported and treated safely away from their

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:44.419
household.

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:48.139
There were congressional briefings on this
just last year in 2019 and a great report

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:52.639
that came out on this called “Flushed and
Forgotten: Sanitation and Wastewater in Rural

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:57.449
Communities in the US,” and that deals with
all sorts of low income communities that tend

00:05:57.449 --> 00:06:02.050
to be Black, Native American and Latinx, and
highlights a lot of the inequalities that

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.100
still exist even in our own country.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:09.860
SIERRA: Do you think you can give me a sort
of run through of how a lack of toilet access

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.120
leads to disease transmission?

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.120
What kinds of disease?

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.740
What are people at risk of here?

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.419
YAMAKOSHI: Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:18.819
So there are a few different ways.

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:25.310
So first of all, I'll talk about the 673 million
people that don't have any type of toilet.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.610
So for those people, their feces are just
in the environment where they live, where

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.460
they work, where their kids are playing, where
maybe they're farming or growing food for

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.699
their own consumption.

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:38.370
Feces in the environment can make it into
their homes, and eventually into their bodies,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.370
through a couple of different pathways.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:44.580
So one is through just normal human behavior,
it could be as simple as walking around outside

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.700
and then tracking that into your home.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.669
Or it could be through the behavior of animals
may be doing the same thing, or even flies

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:52.039
that are going back and forth.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:56.680
SLAYMAKER: But it's not just about whether
I've got a toilet, it's about whether my neighbors

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.229
have got a toilet, and what happens to the
waste from those toilets.

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:05.020
So clearly, once you flush, you can't just
forget about it.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:09.580
You want to be reassured that that waste is
going somewhere, and that it's going to be

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:15.290
treated, and it's not going to just be flushed
into the street, or into someone else's backyard,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:20.300
or into a slum where somebody else's kids
are going to come into contact with the waste.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.990
YAMAKOSHI: Now just a quick run through of
the different diseases that you can get from

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.120
not having a toilet.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.300
So, the big one is diarrhea.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.310
This is a major public health concern.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.430
It's, I believe, the second biggest killer
of children under five.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:39.341
And this category of diarrheal diseases includes
cholera, which is an acute diarrheal disease

00:07:39.341 --> 00:07:40.879
that can kill within hours.

00:07:40.879 --> 00:07:42.710
So, a really big concern.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:45.289
But then there are other neglected tropical
diseases.

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:50.400
So, these are things like worms and trachoma
that can cause a significant burden of disease

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.699
globally, as well as vector borne diseases
that are things you've heard of like West

00:07:54.699 --> 00:08:00.319
Nile Virus that have a more indirect pathway
through sanitation that can facilitate the

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:01.800
breeding of these mosquitoes.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:07.770
You know, really critically for UNICEF, poor
sanitation can lead to malnutrition and stunting,

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:12.879
which is something that can lead to poor physical
and cognitive development of kids and affect

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:14.470
them throughout their entire life.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.800
And there's other things that you've probably
heard of becoming a bigger concern globally,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.069
like antimicrobial resistance.

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:26.020
So poor sanitation can increase the risk of
these preventable infections that we then

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.810
treat with antibiotics.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.580
And then, you know, we are excreting these
sort of organisms into the environment.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.570
So, that's just kind of a run through of some
of the diseases that a lack of toilets can

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:36.570
cause.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:41.650
SIERRA: Sickness and death are only the primary
consequences when people don’t have clean

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:42.769
toilet access.

00:08:42.769 --> 00:08:47.760
Those of you who caught our episode on antibiotic
resistant bacteria know how serious this problem

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.640
can be, and that it doesn’t stay in one
place for very long.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:57.529
And lack of clean and safe toilet access can
also lead to serious and long-term social

00:08:57.529 --> 00:08:58.529
issues.

00:08:58.529 --> 00:09:04.180
SLAYMAKER: If you don't have basic sanitation
facilities in schools, then it's very difficult

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:05.180
to educate children.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:10.810
And if you don't have them in healthcare facilities,
then you can get lots of unintended infections,

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.810
while you're at that health care facility.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.890
But it also goes beyond that, I think.

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:20.710
We would also say that it's about dignity,
and it's about human rights.

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:25.769
YAMAKOSHI: Lack of a toilet can expose women
and girls in particular, although really all

00:09:25.769 --> 00:09:30.990
people to sexual, psychological or physical
abuse or violence.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:35.750
And I mean, it's really important to note
that even just the perceived threat of violence

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.670
or harassment can cause stress.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:43.040
And we all know that just from having to try
to find a public toilet in a park after dark,

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.339
for instance, those of us that live in the
US.

00:09:46.339 --> 00:09:52.540
And this can cause women and children in particular
to not go outside to use a toilet and to engage

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:56.970
in other sorts of behaviors that could be
risky for their health, like holding it or

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:02.070
maybe defecating or urinating in a bucket
until they can empty that outside.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:08.070
Lack of toilets can dissuade women from participating
in the labor force and this has serious consequences

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.019
for all of society and for a country's economic
development.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:18.070
If there aren't toilets in in office buildings,
workplaces, markets, then women may not choose

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:23.529
to work there or may have to cut their hours
short or may miss work on certain days of

00:10:23.529 --> 00:10:27.730
the month for instance, if they're menstruating,
or days of the week if there's something that's

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.000
making them specifically ill.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.399
So this has a cost for all of society.

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:35.440
It's not just about those specific women and
children.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.750
It's something that prevents us as a whole
society from moving forward.

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:44.380
SLAYMAKER: Sanitation is really a sort of
key driver of economic productivity and a

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.089
driver of development.

00:10:46.089 --> 00:10:52.420
So I think, historically, Europe and North
America have made dramatic progress in economic

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:55.160
productivity, following improvements in sanitation.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.700
YAMAKOSHI: So because of this, sanitation
is recognized as a human right and there is

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:05.029
actually a UN General Assembly resolution
on the human right to sanitation, which entitles

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:11.839
everyone to “a physical and affordable access
to sanitation in all spheres of life that

00:11:11.839 --> 00:11:17.700
is safe, hygienic, secure, and socially and
culturally acceptable, and that provides privacy

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.160
and ensures dignity.”

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:23.990
This link between human rights and sanitation
has been made by many who we recognize as

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.209
human rights champions throughout history.

00:11:26.209 --> 00:11:30.760
So this is people like, you know, Mahatma
Gandhi, Nelson Mandela, but also Martin Luther

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:31.839
King, Jr.

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:37.560
SIERRA: Okay so we get how important safe
sanitation is for health, quality of life,

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.420
and economic growth.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.970
But as we discussed at the top of the episode,
solving the problem isn’t as simple as coming

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.920
into an area, installing a few hundred toilets,
and leaving.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.110
The process of changing people's habits is
far more complicated.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:56.589
YAMAKOSHI: Sanitation or using a toilet is
a behavioral pattern that many of us have

00:11:56.589 --> 00:11:58.810
established over many years.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:05.660
So ensuring that everybody, everywhere has
access to safe sanitation is about a few different

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:06.660
things.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:10.100
It's not just the toilet, although the toilet
and the supply is one important component.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:15.459
It's about the behavior, and it's also about
the government policies and the sort of way

00:12:15.459 --> 00:12:20.880
that government can enable sanitation to be
provided to everyone and the view that government

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.610
takes on sanitation being a public good.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:29.010
It's really critical for first time users
of sanitation to do two things that are related

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:30.139
to norms.

00:12:30.139 --> 00:12:35.500
The first is to abandon a longstanding norm
of open defecation.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:40.500
And then it also calls on people to create
a new norm, and that new norm is of consistent

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.040
toilet use.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:45.930
And that can also be challenging, because,
again, this comes back to that it's not only

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:50.410
about the behavior, depending on the type
of facilities that you end up constructing

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:55.990
for yourself, they could be more comfortable
and more desirable than other types of facilities.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:01.430
So it's really important to see how the facilities
can reinforce the behavior.

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:06.050
And the behavior, obviously being at the center
of what it would take to get everybody to

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.550
be able to use a toilet for the first time
everywhere.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:13.840
SLAYMAKER: It's about changing social norms
around sanitation.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:20.860
And making it no longer acceptable to practice
open defecation, and generating an awareness

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:28.269
of the risks associated with unsafe sanitation,
and a sense of pride in having a clean village

00:13:28.269 --> 00:13:32.550
or a clean community or clean country that
other people might want to visit and and invest

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:33.550
in.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:38.089
But the drivers or the incentives, I think,
vary widely.

00:13:38.089 --> 00:13:42.699
So in some countries, you find that, you know,
the main driver is around health, concerns

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:47.670
about health, but in others, it might be around
aspirations of modernity.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:54.240
So, you really have to understand what is
going to motivate people to act and to invest

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:57.750
their own money, but also to work with others
to try and solve the problem.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:01.690
SIERRA: We’re going to take a quick break,
but when we get back we’re going to talk

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:08.339
about India - a country that still accounts
for half of the world’s open defecation.

00:14:08.339 --> 00:14:13.260
Hello listeners!

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.540
So if you dig all the questions we ask in
Why It Matters, you are going to love this

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:21.630
other podcast brought to you by our friends
over at Pushkin Industries.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.990
It’s called Solvable.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.570
Kind of a natural fit.

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:31.199
Solvable is a weekly podcast where Malcolm
Gladwell, Anne Applebaum, and Jacob Weisberg

00:14:31.199 --> 00:14:35.490
ask how we can find solutions to some of the
biggest issues of our time.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:40.620
You’ll hear from David Baltimore on AIDS,
Sal Khan on basic education, and Roseanne

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.780
Hagerty on homelessness.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.630
These problems can seem too big or too complicated
to fix.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:53.820
But in the hands of the right people -- activists,
scientists, policymakers and politicians at

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:57.500
the top of their fields -- there are ways
to solve them.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.829
And that’s the hopeful message of Solvable.

00:14:59.829 --> 00:15:06.010
Listen and subscribe now in your favorite
podcasting app.

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:09.860
When it comes to toilets, the spotlight often
falls on India.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:17.360
According to 2017 statistics from the World
Health Organization and UNICEF, over 542 million

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:24.360
Indians lack basic sanitation, and 345 million
still practice open defecation.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:31.110
The health effects are devastating, including
disease, death, and lifetime debilitations.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:36.050
The last of these has been described as one
of the largest losses of human potential in

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:37.050
history.

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:40.500
India’s problem is not only big, but particularly
hard to solve.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:45.220
And that’s because the country’s sanitation
habits are tied to religious and cultural

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:48.709
beliefs that have existed for thousands of
years.

00:15:48.709 --> 00:15:55.810
VYAS: You can't really talk about open defecation
without talking about India.

00:15:55.810 --> 00:16:03.769
More than half of rural Indians still go in
the open based on the most recent credible

00:16:03.769 --> 00:16:06.839
estimates of you know, where people poop.

00:16:06.839 --> 00:16:08.839
SIERRA: This is Sangita Vyas.

00:16:08.839 --> 00:16:13.769
She’s a researcher that focuses on environmental
risks to child health in India.

00:16:13.769 --> 00:16:20.950
VYAS: So, to understand why open defecation
persists we have to sort of understand a little

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:27.560
bit about how people think about purity and
pollution in India.

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:30.910
So why is this important for sanitation?

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:36.940
Well, it's because these ideas of purity and
pollution map on to the caste system.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:38.440
So, what is caste?

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:44.009
Well, caste has its roots in Hinduism and
it's basically a social hierarchy.

00:16:44.009 --> 00:16:46.399
It's something that you're born into.

00:16:46.399 --> 00:16:50.960
You can't change caste much like you can't
change race.

00:16:50.960 --> 00:17:00.449
Now, higher caste people are seen to be more
pure than lower caste people and people from

00:17:00.449 --> 00:17:05.990
the very lowest castes, which historically
were called Untouchables and now they are

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:12.510
called Dalits, they are seen to be permanently
polluted and unfortunately they are extremely

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:18.610
marginalized and oppressed in Indian society
even today.

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:28.020
Now, the Dalit community they have traditionally
been responsible for doing the work called

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.760
manual scavenging.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:33.169
This is the daily cleaning of feces from a
dry toilet.

00:17:33.169 --> 00:17:40.740
And because people from this particular caste
had to do this work, were compelled to do

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:45.620
this work, they were seen as polluted by this
work.

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:51.320
SIERRA: Over time, as opportunities have slowly
grown, members of the Dalit caste have become

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:53.640
less willing to carry out this work.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:58.370
This means that many Indians in rural communities
have been left to clean their own latrines.

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:02.559
If the act of emptying your latrine would
cause you to become untouchable within the

00:18:02.559 --> 00:18:06.010
caste system, chances are you’d avoid doing
it.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:11.080
As a result, large pit latrines and open defecation
are seen as good alternatives.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:16.810
Yet, exposure to waste is causing Indians,
and particularly Indian children, incredible

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:22.750
harm, including mental and physical damage
that will remain with them all their lives.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:28.530
Of these non-lethal effects, the most dramatic
is stunting, which affects some 65 million

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.530
Indian children under the age of five.

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:35.870
It has been described as one of the largest
losses of human potential in history.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:39.610
It’s a problem that the Indian government
has tried to address.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:45.800
VYAS: So the Indian government started this
campaign in 2014.

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:52.030
And the goal was to eliminate open defecation
in a five year timeframe.

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:58.559
And the vast majority of that work and the
money spent on this campaign went towards

00:18:58.559 --> 00:19:01.380
latrine construction.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:09.180
Now what I've just told you about is that
it's much less about economic status and having

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:11.050
the money to construct a toilet.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:18.340
It's much more about attitudes and norms and
beliefs that really promote open defecation

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:21.820
and prevent people from wanting to use the
latrine.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:28.860
So, you would think that, well, maybe it needs
to be more about promotional campaigns to

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:35.200
try to convince people that going in the latrine
is good and pure and doesn't create challenges

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.390
for observing purity.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:43.970
And unfortunately, that wasn't really what
was focused on in the campaign, in the government

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:44.970
campaign.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:51.020
So it was just much more about construction,
much less about convincing people that using

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.890
a latrine is a good thing.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:59.760
And that there are ways to empty the latrine
that don't pose challenges for observing purity,

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:01.240
and things like that.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:07.059
SIERRA: India’s ruling party claims to have
constructed more than 110 million toilets

00:20:07.059 --> 00:20:11.360
as part of this effort, and made them accessible
to 600 million people.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:15.880
And while this number is impressive, Sangita
and other researchers believe there is still

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:20.890
a great amount of work to be done, not only
to maintain these facilities, but also in

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:22.360
the field of education.

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:30.560
VYAS: So, the government very much believes
that this program was successful and that

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:35.919
there's not really a need to keep doing this
government campaign anymore.

00:20:35.919 --> 00:20:40.380
The political will to work on sanitation is
now over.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:42.890
Does open defecation still persist?

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:44.900
Yes, it does.

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:52.370
A number of studies that include work that
my colleagues and I have done, include work

00:20:52.370 --> 00:21:00.610
that other researchers and teams have done,
really show that many people still defecate

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:06.190
in the open and that the problem is far from
being solved.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:11.460
Our research also shows that many households
now have a latrine.

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:16.630
And so the Swachh Bharat mission really did
seem to actually succeed in getting households

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.040
to construct latrines.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:22.559
But there's still a barrier in terms of people
using the latrine.

00:21:22.559 --> 00:21:28.420
You know, these are beliefs that have been
held for a very long time.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:37.110
They are deeply ingrained and they're kind
of similar to race in America.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:47.250
I think that solving the sanitation problem
in India would require really attacking caste,

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.790
really attacking untouchability.

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:55.929
And if people didn't believe in untouchability
or didn't believe in purity anymore, then

00:21:55.929 --> 00:21:59.510
they wouldn't mind using the toilet, but it's
gonna be really tough to get people to not

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:01.779
believe in purity anymore.

00:22:01.779 --> 00:22:07.890
SIERRA: Open defecation is a public health
issue that holds India back even as the country

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:10.240
grows in so many other ways.

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:14.320
Breaking old cultural barriers is a slow,
difficult task.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:18.360
But that doesn’t mean that the experts who
have dedicated their lives to fixing this

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:20.270
problem are giving up.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:25.559
Even as challenges like India loom large,
there have also been success cases.

00:22:25.559 --> 00:22:30.710
YAMAKOSHI: Eliminating open defecation by
2030, which is the year that the global community

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:36.070
has marked as the target for getting this
done, will require a massive acceleration

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:41.540
in toilet use and a massive acceleration beyond
the rate at which we've been progressing.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:46.690
One of the key factors in rapid progress all
over the world, where we've seen societies

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:52.169
achieve near total sanitation in a generation
is strong government leadership.

00:22:52.169 --> 00:22:56.840
Strong government leadership that prioritizes
sanitation and celebrates things like World

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:01.059
Toilet Day, despite it perhaps sounding a
little bit funny.

00:23:01.059 --> 00:23:06.670
So maybe just looking back to the 1960s and
the 1970s, there were four countries in East

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:10.660
Asia, and those were Singapore, Thailand,
South Korea and Malaysia.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:15.730
And they were able to produce really remarkable
results in a generation.

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:20.010
And the interesting thing about those countries
is that while now we think of them as being

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:25.900
wealthy countries, at the time, the per capita
income levels in those East Asian states were

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:30.890
equivalent to the income levels of many Sub
Saharan African countries today.

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:34.279
So that just shows that even in a resource
constrained environment, you can make a lot

00:23:34.279 --> 00:23:35.279
of progress.

00:23:35.279 --> 00:23:39.929
Since 2000, there are a couple of states that
have made rapid progress in eliminating open

00:23:39.929 --> 00:23:44.160
defecation or reducing open defecation to
much lower levels.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:50.419
So Ethiopia, Cambodia, both of those countries
reduced open defecation by more than 50 percentage

00:23:50.419 --> 00:23:51.419
points.

00:23:51.419 --> 00:23:56.020
And then there are other countries like Laos,
Nepal and Indonesia, that have been able to

00:23:56.020 --> 00:24:02.270
increase the use of toilets to more than 40
percentage points and those are decent toilets

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:05.960
that are separating waste from human contact.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:09.880
And to highlight another country that is a
low income country is Burkina Faso.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:14.900
Since 2000, Burkina Faso has reduced open
defecation by 25 percentage points, which

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.679
is really incredible.

00:24:16.679 --> 00:24:21.549
SIERRA: Experts agree that strong, sustained
support from the government is an essential

00:24:21.549 --> 00:24:23.930
factor in solving the problem.

00:24:23.930 --> 00:24:28.190
But when it comes to the nuts and bolts, there
is no single message or technique that will

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:29.890
work on a global scale.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:33.840
And that’s because the cultural and behavioral
challenges are different depending on where

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:34.840
you are.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:40.510
SLAYMAKER: A lot depends on, you know, the
way people are living, the type of housing

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:41.570
they have.

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:44.200
Whether they're living in urban areas or rural
areas.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:49.960
So really, it's very important to have that
local knowledge and that's why having country

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:55.559
offices who are very embedded in the local
culture and in the local economy, trying to

00:24:55.559 --> 00:25:00.270
figure out what is going to be the best combination
of interventions in order to move things forward

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:01.590
on the ground.

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:06.490
SIERRA: I just find myself learning so much
from this conversation and it makes me wonder

00:25:06.490 --> 00:25:09.730
if there's enough global awareness about this
problem?

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:14.570
SLAYMAKER: Well, I think for those of us who
work on it, we do get frustrated that it's

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:18.010
not taken more seriously or or given more
political attention.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:22.730
I think one of the things we find is that
the first thing that a politician will notice

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:27.309
when they visit a community or visit a country
is the sanitation facilities.

00:25:27.309 --> 00:25:32.409
If the toilets are in a terrible condition,
they'll complain but it's also the first thing

00:25:32.409 --> 00:25:36.529
that they forget when they leave, and they
get in the car, or they get in the plane and

00:25:36.529 --> 00:25:37.529
go away.

00:25:37.529 --> 00:25:41.640
And I think one of the reasons for that is
that, you know, sanitation is just not a very

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:42.799
sexy topic.

00:25:42.799 --> 00:25:48.670
And so, politicians would much prefer to be
opening a school or opening a new health care

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:53.370
facility than cutting the ribbon on a new
fecal sludge treatment facility, for example.

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:57.429
But there all, you know, examples of where
governments have really stepped up and shown

00:25:57.429 --> 00:26:02.210
leadership and recognized the importance of
the issue, and it shows that it can be done.

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:07.540
SIERRA: When we think of things someone needs
in order to have a chance at a decent life,

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:11.809
we think of food, education, healthcare, freedom.

00:26:11.809 --> 00:26:16.399
It’s strange at first to add the word “toilet”
to the list, but, you know, the more you think

00:26:16.399 --> 00:26:22.540
about it, and the more you consider the consequences,
toilets really do emerge as a basic human

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:23.799
need.

00:26:23.799 --> 00:26:28.710
The desire to end death and disease due to
poor sanitation is reason enough to solve

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:29.850
the problem.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:32.159
But it’s also not the only reason.

00:26:32.159 --> 00:26:36.120
The health effects for those who lack toilet
access can easily spill over to the rest of

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:42.029
the world’s population, through resurgent
diseases and antibiotic resistant bacteria.

00:26:42.029 --> 00:26:46.779
Lack of access also slows development and
harms innovation in countries like India with

00:26:46.779 --> 00:26:50.909
which the United States needs strong strategic
partnerships.

00:26:50.909 --> 00:26:57.260
As mundane as they seem, toilets are simply
a key part of developing a fair, peaceful,

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:59.070
and prosperous world.

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:04.280
And yet for all that,they are rarely front
and center when people talk about global health.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:07.289
And that has to change.

00:27:07.289 --> 00:27:09.049
SIERRA: So.

00:27:09.049 --> 00:27:13.559
If you could say one thing to someone who
has never thought about this issue before,

00:27:13.559 --> 00:27:14.620
what would it be?

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:17.480
YAMAKOSHI: That's a really good question.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:24.230
I would say that just because you have sanitation,
it doesn't mean that you're safe.

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:27.090
It's not enough for it to just be you.

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:28.400
Sanitation is a public good.

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.659
It requires everyone to have it.

00:27:30.659 --> 00:27:33.020
It requires your neighbor to have it for your
own health.

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:37.340
It requires all of society to have it for
a country to be able to develop.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:41.519
I know that these notions of modernity and
using the word “modern” can sometimes

00:27:41.519 --> 00:27:47.230
be loaded, but I would challenge you to name
one country that you think of as being advanced

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:52.450
or modern that doesn't have universal access
to safe sanitation.

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.350
In that way, sanitation is a solution.

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:57.840
It can help to achieve all of these other
goals.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:04.380
Goals in health, in gender equality, poverty
eradication, education, having clean environments,

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:08.380
and food security, and so many more other
goals that I'm forgetting.

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:13.510
So if we want to live in these clean and healthy
homes for ourselves, if we want our children

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:17.900
to go to schools that are safe and healthy
learning environments, if we want to go to

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:23.320
the hospital, and be able to relieve ourselves
inside, we need to give this issue priority.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:28.049
And the last thing I'll say about this is
sanitation is really a no regrets investment.

00:28:28.049 --> 00:28:34.279
I would really challenge all of us to find
one government or one individual person that

00:28:34.279 --> 00:28:43.040
invested in sanitation and regretted it.

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:53.909
